{"@odata.context":"$metadata#Directions/$entity","booleans":{},"counters":{},"entity_id":"en-019d95ab-e27b-7e42-b20f-b72c39a55483","entity_type":"Direction","events":[{"action":"Created","from_status":"","params":{},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T09:42:43.068461Z","to_status":"Proposed"},{"action":"Propose","from_status":"Proposed","params":{"counterfactual_summary":"If organizations can scale agent use inside current structures with little process change, then adoption will broaden faster and the workforce mix will stay more stable than this direction predicts.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d95ab-5baa-7fa3-87fe-ac2cecf9c69c", "en-019d95ab-7ee1-79e0-83ab-c9c29f3480ac"]","projection_id":"en-019d95aa-b3d9-77c3-8da3-2d8b6860d48b","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d95aa-b43d-70d2-a81c-e1c9035f57dc","reasoning":"The critical adoption barrier for directed software evolution is not whether agents can generate multiple viable code variants; it is whether organizations can absorb those variants inside existing approval, testing, and accountability systems. In most enterprises, architecture review boards, QA processes, ticketing norms, and managerial incentives were designed for human-paced software production. When agents accelerate generation without equivalent changes to review rights, ownership boundaries, and performance metrics, the new throughput is converted into queue length. This is why pilot success will often fail to scale into portfolio-level delivery improvement.

